include 'lib/sdf.limut'

// 3d signed distance fields and a raymarcher for visualsynth px chains, converted into limut DSL
// from the GLSL on Inigo Quilez's page: https://iquilezles.org/articles/distfunctions/
//
// A 3d distance field is the same idea as the 2d ones in lib/sdf.limut - a number per point giving
// the distance to the nearest surface, negative inside - but of a point in space rather than on the
// plane, so it describes a solid. Turning one into a picture is a bigger step than thresholding it:
// a ray is fired into the scene for every pixel and walked along until it lands on a surface, which
// is what sd3march does. That works because a distance field says how far it is safe to step.
//
//   v1 visualsynth, px=sd3march>>sd3lit
//   set ring = pxfn{{p}->sd3torus{p}}
//   v1 visualsynth, px=sd3march{ring, yaw:[0:1]l8}>>sd3lit{ring}
//
// Everything here is an ordinary user defined function built out of the visual nodes; nothing is
// implemented in javascript. It includes lib/sdf.limut, so the 2d shapes, the combining operators
// they share (sdround, sdannular, sdsub, sdblend) and the whole Visual Library come with it.
//
// The scene wants to be a pxfn{}, ie a real function in the generated shader. A march calls it once
// per step, the surface normal calls it four more times, and a shadow or an occlusion term calls it
// again at every one of their steps; without pxfn{} each of those is another copy of the whole
// scene written into the shader, which is what exhausts a small GPU's register allocator. With it
// there is one copy and many calls:
//
//   set scene = pxfn{{p} -> min{ sd3sphere{p, r:1/2}, sd3plane{p} }}
//   v1 visualsynth, px=sd3march{scene}>>sd3lit{scene}
//
// Or declared inline with let{}, on the line that marches it. A pxfn{} is the one thing let binds as
// the function it is rather than as the value it takes where the let is written, so the name reaches
// every use site the way a `set` name does - and it is the tighter of the two spellings, since it is
// one node shared by all of them rather than one worked out afresh at each: measured on sd3torus,
// one declaration called ten times against the set form's five copies, 61 uniforms against 69, and
// the two render identically to the byte. Without the pxfn{} the let means what it always does, the
// value at that point in the chain - for a scene, the slice through the chain head, which the march
// then steps through as a constant.
//
//   v1 visualsynth, px=let{'scene', pxfn{sd3torus}}>>sd3march{scene}>>sd3lit{scene}
//
// The scene is declared first on every function that takes one, so it is the argument a bare name
// goes to, the way shape is on kal; `in` still takes the piped value wherever it is declared. That
// matters here more than usual, because a call whose own args already hold a visual node is not
// piped the chain value at all - so with `in` declared first, px=sd3march{scene} would have put the
// scene in `in` and marched the default.
//
// A scene is a function of the point alone: a pxfn{} body sees its argument, uv, and the animated
// params, but not a let{} from outside it nor the index of a loop{} it sits inside. Animating one
// is therefore fine - a timevar in a scene is a param like any other - but anything positional has
// to arrive through the point.
//
// The coordinate system is the px one carried into three dimensions: y is up, x is right, and the
// frame spans -1 to 1 vertically (wider horizontally, by the aspect ratio). z goes away from the
// viewer, so the default camera sits at z=-3 and looks toward the origin. Distances and radii are
// in those same units throughout.
//
// Two things about the shader shape of all this. There are no branches, so every `if` in the
// original becomes the ternary `cond ?? a ?: b`, which compiles to a mix between both results
// rather than taking one path - which also means both sides are always worked out. And a value
// wanted twice is either named with a let{} or passed on to a helper function, whose argument names
// it; an expression on its own has nowhere to put a local, but a chain does, and a let{} is a chain
// stage. The marches here use let{}, and a carried one at that, since their values change every
// iteration, which is exactly what a loop{}'s carry: is for. The shapes use helpers, for the reason
// lib/sdf.limut gives: a helper's argument is already one built value and so costs nothing, where a
// let{} only pays when an expression rather than a value is what is wanted twice.

//// Machinery

// A 3d point: w has to be zero, since length, dot and cross work on xyz and the incoming w is 1,
// and a stray w would survive every translate to turn up in the next length.
set sd3flat = {in} -> in >> set{w:0}

// Build a 3d point out of three computed values. Each of those arrives splatted across all four
// channels (as every distance here does), so the masks pick one channel out of each.
set sd3vec = {a, b, c} -> {x:1,w:0}*a + {y:1,w:0}*b + {z:1,w:0}*c

// Force a colour opaque. Shading multiplies a colour by a brightness, and a brightness is a single
// value in all four channels, so without this the alpha comes out dimmed along with the rest.
// It is a chain stage rather than the colour arithmetic it looks like it could be, because that
// arithmetic on two constants has no visual node in it: the whole thing const folds, and a folded
// colour times a channel map is a value toVec4 cannot read, so it comes back as the flat white
// fallback (measured 2026-09-13: px=#0f0*{x:1,y:1,z:1,w:0} is a white frame). set{} is a real node,
// so it works whatever it is handed - which is the general rule here, and the reason sd3vec and the
// masks below are only ever used on values that already have a node in them.
set sd3opaque = {in:id} -> in >> set{w:1}

// Reflect a direction about a unit normal. GLSL has reflect() as an intrinsic; the px maths
// functions do not, and it is one line.
set sd3reflect = {i, n} -> i - n*2*dot{i,n}

// The 2d radial distance from the y axis, ie how far out from the middle a point is. Several of the
// shapes below are a 2d shape taken in that radius and y, which is what makes them solids of
// revolution. It is spelt out rather than using length{} because length{} is over xyz.
set sd3radial = {p} -> length{sd3vec{p.x, p.z, 0}}

//// Shapes
// All of them take the incoming px value as their first argument, so they pipe like any other chain
// segment and can equally be handed a point of their own - which is what a scene does:
// pxfn{{p} -> sd3sphere{p, r:1/2}}. As in the 2d library, a positional argument only lands where you
// expect when the call is being piped one, so name them anywhere else.

// Distance from the origin, less the radius: the simplest field there is, and exact everywhere.
set sd3sphere = {in:id, r:1/2} -> sdval{length{sd3flat{in}} - r}

// Box of half extents b, ie b is the distance from the middle to a face, not the width. A plain
// number gives a cube; a vector gives a cuboid. The outside distance is the length of the positive
// part of |p|-b (which covers the corner and edge cases as much as the face case) and the inside
// distance is the largest of the three negative components; only one of the two terms is ever
// non zero.
set sd3box1 = {q} -> sdval{length{max{q,0}} + min{max{q.x, q.y, q.z}, 0}}
set sd3box = {in:id, b:1/2} -> sd3box1{abs{sd3flat{in}} - b}

// Torus lying in the xz plane: r is the radius of the ring measured to the middle of the tube, t
// the radius of the tube itself. The radial distance less r, paired with y, is a 2d point whose
// length is the distance from the ring.
set sd3torus1 = {q, t} -> sdval{length{q} - t}
set sd3torus = {in:id, r:1/2, t:1/8} ->
  sd3torus1{sd3vec{sd3radial{in} - r, in.y, 0}, t}

// Capsule: a line from a to b with a radius around it, ie a cylinder with hemispherical ends.
// Project the point onto the segment, clamp the projection into it, and measure from there.
set sd3capsule1 = {pa, ba, r} -> sdval{length{pa - ba*clamp{dot{pa,ba}/dot{ba,ba}}} - r}
set sd3capsule = {in:id, ax:0, ay:-1/2, az:0, bx:0, by:1/2, bz:0, r:1/4} ->
  sd3capsule1{sd3flat{in} - {x:ax, y:ay, z:az, w:0}, {x:bx-ax, y:by-ay, z:bz-az, w:0}, r}

// Cylinder of radius r and half height h, standing on the y axis with flat ends. The radius and y
// make a 2d point, and the rest is the 2d box of half extents r,h taken on it.
set sd3cyl1 = {d} -> sdval{min{max{d.x, d.y}, 0} + length{max{d, 0}}}
set sd3cylinder = {in:id, r:1/2, h:1/2} ->
  sd3cyl1{abs{sd3vec{sd3radial{in}, in.y, 0}} - {x:r, y:h, w:0}}

// Half space below the plane through the origin with unit normal nx,ny,nz, lifted by h. The default
// is the ground: a floor at y = -h. The normal must be a unit vector, since the distance is measured
// along it.
set sd3plane = {in:id, nx:0, ny:1, nz:0, h:1/2} ->
  sdval{dot{sd3flat{in}, {x:nx, y:ny, z:nz, w:0}} + h}

// Octahedron of radius r (the distance from the middle to a point). This is Inigo Quilez's bound
// rather than his exact field: it is correct on the surface and never overestimates, so a march
// lands in the right place, but it is not a true distance away from it - which shows if you round
// or blend it. 0.57735027 is one over the square root of 3.
set sd3octahedron = {in:id, r:1/2} ->
  sdval{(abs{in.x} + abs{in.y} + abs{in.z} - r) * 0.57735027}

//// Shapes from 2d ones
// These two make a solid out of any field from lib/sdf.limut, which is the cheapest way to get a
// shape that is not in the list above. The 2d shape is a chain of its own, so it is passed in as an
// argument and reached as a bare name (sdstar{r:1/2} works, since its own `in` defaults to whatever
// arrives); pxfn{} around it is worth it only if the same shape is used more than once.

// Extrude a 2d shape along z into a prism of half thickness h. The 2d field is taken in xy.
//   px=sd3march{scene:pxfn{{p}->sd3extrude{p, sdstar{r:1/2}, h:1/8}}}>>sd3lit
set sd3extrude1 = {w} -> sdval{min{max{w.x, w.y}, 0} + length{max{w, 0}}}
set sd3extrude = {in:id, shape, h:1/4} ->
  sd3extrude1{sd3vec{sd3vec{in.x, in.y, 0} >> shape, abs{in.z} - h, 0}}

// Revolve a 2d shape about the y axis, o being how far out from the axis the shape's own origin is
// placed. The 2d field is taken in the radial distance and y, so sd3revolve with a circle is a
// torus and with a box is a tube.
//   px=sd3march{scene:pxfn{{p}->sd3revolve{p, sdheart{r:1/3}, o:1/2}}}>>sd3lit
set sd3revolve = {in:id, shape, o:1/2} ->
  sd3vec{sd3radial{in} - o, in.y, 0} >> shape

//// Combining
// Union and intersection of two distances are min{} and max{}, and sdround, sdannular, sdsub and
// sdblend from lib/sdf.limut all work on a 3d distance unchanged, since all of those are arithmetic
// on the distance rather than on the point. Only the ones that are about being in three dimensions
// are here.
// There is no per shape material. It was built and then taken out again: tagging a distance and
// picking the nearer tag both work, but the colouring step does not, because a loop{} carry: name
// the march assigns is readable from the body of a later function and not from an argument written
// at its callsite. An ordinary let{} name reads fine in either place; it is the carried one that
// does not. See ToDo.txt.

// Repeat the whole of space on a lattice, so one shape becomes endlessly many. p is the spacing,
// either a number for all three axes or a vector; every component must be non zero, since the point
// is divided by it.
//   px=sd3march{scene:pxfn{{p}->sd3sphere{sd3rep{p, 3/2}, r:1/2}}, far:32}>>sd3lit
set sd3rep = {in:id, p:1} -> sd3flat{in} - p*round{sd3flat{in}/p}

// (Mirroring space into the positive octant with abs{} belongs here too, and is not, because it
// would not render: see ToDo.txt.)

//// Marching
// The ray for this pixel: the point on an image plane at z = fov, normalised. A larger fov is a
// longer lens, ie a narrower field of view and less perspective.
set sd3dir = {in:id, fov:1} -> normalize{sd3vec{in.x, in.y, fov}}

// The default scene, so that px=sd3march>>sd3lit is a picture rather than an error: a sphere on a
// floor. It is a pxfn{} for the reason the header gives, and everything it needs is written inside
// it because it takes no arguments.
set sd3default = pxfn{{p} -> min{sd3sphere{p, 1/2}, sd3plane{p, h:1/2}}}

// Walk a ray from the camera into the scene until it lands on a surface. The point the ray reached
// is what comes out, so the shading after it works on a point in space; what happened on the way is
// read back by name afterwards - sd3d is the scene's last result, ie the distance in x; sd3t is how
// far the ray travelled, for depth and fog; and sd3n is the surface normal where it stopped, which
// is what everything about light needs.
// The normal is worked out here, once, rather than by each of the shading functions separately -
// they would each want the same one, and it is four more calls of the scene every time. The cost is
// that a render wanting no light at all still asks for it; the four calls have no other effect, so
// a shader compiler is free to drop them, but they are in the generated source either way.
// sd3lit reads all three, and so can anything of your own.
//
// The camera sits at ox,oy,oz and looks along +z, turned by pitch then yaw, both in turns like
// rot2. steps is how many times the ray may step and is structural - it is the loop count written
// into the shader, so it can be changed but not smoothly animated. eps is how close counts as a
// hit and far is where to give up; the loop stops at whichever comes first, which is what keeps a
// march affordable, since the background and the near hits each come in large runs of pixels.
//
// sd3rd is named before the loop so the ray direction is worked out once rather than at every step.
// The body names the distance before stepping by it, which is a let{} of an expression and so needs
// no id>> of its own; the one in front is the explicit seed any body starting with a plain call
// wants. There is no `and` or `or` in a condition - comparisons give 1 or 0, so multiply them for
// and and add them for or, which is what the two ways of finishing are here.
set sd3march = {scene:sd3default, in:id, ox:0, oy:0, oz:-3, fov:1, yaw:0, pitch:0,
                steps:64, eps:1/2000, far:16} ->
  in >> let{sd3rd, rot3y{rot3x{sd3dir{id, fov}, pitch}, yaw}}
     >> set{x:ox, y:oy, z:oz, w:0}
     >> loop{
          {i} -> id >> let{sd3d, scene} >> let{sd3t, sd3t + sd3d.x} >> add{sd3rd*sd3d.x},
          steps,
          carry:{sd3d:far, sd3t:0},
          until: (sd3d.x < eps) + (sd3t.x > far)
        }
     >> let{sd3n, sd3nrm{scene, id}}

//// Shading
// The surface normal is the direction the field increases in fastest, ie its gradient. It is taken
// as four samples around the point, at the corners of a tetrahedron - the fewest that span three
// dimensions - which is four more calls of the scene and the main reason a scene wants to be a
// pxfn{}. h is how far apart the samples are: too small and it is noise, too large and it rounds
// off every edge. sd3march calls this once and leaves the answer in sd3n, so the shading functions
// below take no normal of their own; call it directly only for a normal somewhere else.
set sd3tet = {e, p, scene, h} -> e * ((p + e*h) >> scene).x
set sd3nrm = {scene:sd3default, in:id, h:1/2000} -> normalize{
    sd3tet{{x:1,y:-1,z:-1,w:0}, in, scene, h}
  + sd3tet{{x:-1,y:-1,z:1,w:0}, in, scene, h}
  + sd3tet{{x:-1,y:1,z:-1,w:0}, in, scene, h}
  + sd3tet{{x:1,y:1,z:1,w:0}, in, scene, h}
}

// A unit vector toward the light, shared by the shading functions so that one set of lx, ly, lz
// means the same thing to all of them
set sd3lightdir = {lx, ly, lz} -> normalize{{x:lx, y:ly, z:lz, w:0}}

// How much of the light reaches this point: march a second ray from the surface toward the light
// and see whether anything is in the way, giving 1 in full light and 0 in full shadow. k is the
// softness - the edge is blurred by the closest the ray came to something relative to how far it
// had gone, so a smaller k is a softer edge and a large one is nearly hard. Starting a little way
// along the normal is what stops the surface from shadowing itself.
// It is the same shape as sd3march with one more thing carried: sd3sr, the darkest it got. The loop
// hands on its point as always, so the answer is read back off the name afterwards, which is all
// sd3shadow1 is for.
//   px=sd3march{scene}>>sd3lit{scene, shadow:sd3shadow{scene}}
set sd3shadow1 = {in} -> clamp{sd3sr}
set sd3shadow = {scene:sd3default, in:id, lx:1/2, ly:1, lz:-1/2,
                 k:8, steps:24, mint:1/64, far:8} ->
  (sd3flat{in} + sd3n*mint) >> let{sd3sl, sd3lightdir{lx, ly, lz}}
    >> loop{
         {i} -> id >> let{sd3sd, scene}
                   >> let{sd3sr, min{sd3sr, k*sd3sd.x/sd3st.x}}
                   >> let{sd3st, sd3st + clamp{sd3sd.x, 1/64, 1/4}}
                   >> add{sd3sl*clamp{sd3sd.x, 1/64, 1/4}},
         steps,
         carry:{sd3sd:far, sd3sr:1, sd3st:mint},
         until: (sd3sr.x < 1/1000) + (sd3st.x > far)
       }
    >> sd3shadow1

// Ambient occlusion: how much of the sky this point can see, approximated by asking how much nearer
// the surface is than it would be on an open plane, at a few points out along the normal. Nothing
// is carried from one sample to the next, so this is a fold rather than a march - a loop{} with
// only a map:, whose terms are summed. steps of 4 gives 5 samples, since a fold takes a term at
// every value the loop visits, the seed included.
//   px=sd3march{scene}>>sd3lit{scene, occ:sd3ao{scene}}
set sd3aostep = {i, in, n, scene, hstep, falloff} ->
  (hstep*(i+1) - ((in + n*hstep*(i+1)) >> scene).x) * (falloff^i)
set sd3ao = {scene:sd3default, in:id, steps:4, hstep:1/32, falloff:0.95, k:3} ->
  clamp{1 - k*loop{map:{v,i} -> sd3aostep{i, sd3flat{in}, sd3n, scene, hstep, falloff}, steps}}

// Turn the point a march landed on into a colour. It reads sd3d, sd3t and sd3n, the names sd3march
// leaves behind, so it goes after one; there is nothing for it to shade before the march. A miss is
// the background, a hit is the surface colour lit by one light plus an ambient term, and fog fades
// the whole thing toward the background with distance (0, the default, is none).
// shadow and occ are plain multipliers on the two terms, so the cheap picture really is cheap: both
// sides of a ternary are always compiled, so an "off" flag would not have saved anything, where a
// multiplier of 1 costs nothing. Pass sd3shadow and sd3ao into them for the expensive one.
//   px=sd3march{scene}>>sd3lit{scene}
//   px=sd3march{scene}>>sd3lit{scene, shadow:sd3shadow{scene}, occ:sd3ao{scene}}
set sd3lit = {scene:sd3default, in:id, lx:1/2, ly:1, lz:-1/2, col:#fff, bg:#123,
              amb:1/5, shadow:1, occ:1, eps:1/500, fog:0} ->
  ( sd3d.x < eps
    ?? mix{
         col * (amb*occ + shadow*max{dot{sd3n, sd3lightdir{lx, ly, lz}}, 0}),
         bg,
         clamp{sd3t*fog}
       }
    ?: bg
  ) >> set{w:1}
